# React + TypeScript Refactoring Standards

Apply these standards when refactoring existing code. Preserve behavior unless explicitly asked to change it.

## Core Refactor Principles
- Keep user-facing behavior unchanged by default.
- Prefer small, reversible steps over large rewrites.
- Move business logic out of giant components into hooks/services.
- Keep module boundaries explicit: UI should not own data orchestration and transport details.

## Component and Hook Structure
- Target component size under ~200-250 lines when practical.
- Split large screens into composition + focused child sections.
- Split "god hooks" by concern (data loading, mutations, side effects, derived state).
- Keep file exports narrow and intentional (avoid accidental deep coupling).

## TypeScript Quality
- Avoid `any`; prefer `unknown` + explicit narrowing.
- Model state with unions where states are mutually exclusive.
- Export reusable contracts near domain boundaries.
- Keep transformation logic typed (DTO -> view model).

## Error and UX Safety
- Do not silently swallow critical errors.
- For user-significant failures: provide feedback (toast/block + retry).
- Keep loading/success/error/empty states explicit for lists and async UI.
- Reuse shared UI primitives for consistency (empty/skeleton/error patterns).

## Performance During Refactor
- Memoize only proven hotspots (`memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`).
- Avoid recreating heavy objects/functions on every render.
- Keep expensive work outside render where possible.
- Use lazy loading/code-splitting for heavy route-level chunks.

## Refactor Checklist
- [ ] Behavior preserved (or intentionally changed and documented)
- [ ] Duplicate logic reduced
- [ ] File complexity reduced
- [ ] Types improved (no new `any`)
- [ ] Error handling explicit
- [ ] Loading/error/empty states present where needed
- [ ] No new cross-feature boundary violations
